findings.body:
`asize` is seeded with the libc symbol instead of Redis' allocator abstraction:

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1214-1217"}

`zmalloc_size()` (`src/zmalloc.h`) is not a plain wrapper — it maps to `je_malloc_usable_size` under jemalloc, `tc_malloc_size` under tcmalloc, `malloc_size` on Apple, and to an internal header-aware function when no usable-size API exists. It is also what `zfree()` uses to account frees. The original PR description explicitly asks for `zmalloc_size`.

::callout{type="warning"}
Default Linux builds use jemalloc (`MALLOC=jemalloc`, `src/Makefile:80-82`) configured with `--with-jemalloc-prefix=je_` (`deps/Makefile:141`). In that configuration `zmalloc.h` never includes `<malloc.h>`, so `malloc_usable_size` is an implicit declaration — a hard compile error in the CI jobs that pass `-Werror`. Even without `-Werror`, the call binds to glibc's implementation while the pointer was allocated by `je_malloc`, an out-of-bounds read of allocator metadata that returns garbage or crashes. Builds without the symbol (macOS, `NO_MALLOC_USABLE_SIZE`) fail to link.

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1217"}
size_t asize = zmalloc_size((void *)o);

`elesize` is the bytes of the sampled quicklist *nodes*; `elecount` is the number of *elements* in those nodes, and `ql->count` is the total element count. The extrapolation therefore has to divide by `elecount`:

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1234-1239"}

Dividing by `samples` (number of nodes) yields bytes-per-node, then multiplies by the total number of elements. With the default `sample_size` of 5 and nodes holding ~100 entries, MEMORY USAGE over-reports list memory by roughly two orders of magnitude. `elecount` is still accumulated on line 1236 but is now never read.

Concrete example: one node with 100 small entries whose node+listpack allocation is ~700 bytes and `ql->count == 100`. Correct: `700/100*100 = 700`. New: `700/1*100 = 70000`.

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1239"}
asize += (double)elesize/elecount*ql->count;

When `numele > samples`, `lpsize` is reduced to the average listpack size and used to estimate the first `numele-1` listpacks; the actual size of the last listpack is then added on line 1336:

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1326-1337"}

Multiplying by `numele` counts that last listpack in the average as well, so it is counted twice. This contradicts the comment immediately above (lines 1311-1314) and the `numele <= samples` fast path. With 6 listpacks and the default 5 samples the result is over-reported by roughly one full listpack (about 17% in that case).

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1330"}
asize += lpsize * (s->rax->numele - 1);

The hash/list edits only rename a comment; coverage is still `> 0`:

::diff{file="tests/unit/type/list.tcl" lines="2244-2245"}

On top of that, `memory_usage` (`tests/support/util.tcl:1152-1160`) returns a constant `1` for non-jemalloc allocators, so on the `make MALLOC=libc` CI job these assertions can never distinguish a correct value from a wildly wrong one. The new string test is the only value-based test, and it exercises none of the quicklist or stream sampling code — exactly the code whose math this PR changes. Add tests that build a list spanning several quicklist nodes and a stream with more listpacks than the sample size, and assert reported usage is within a sane band of the real payload. Also add `assert_encoding` to the string test so EMBSTR/RAW/INT are actually exercised.

::callout{type="warning"}
This gap is why both arithmetic regressions above pass the test suite.
